WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
If you already had multiple real estate

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.280
companies doing millions of dollars a

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.360
year, why would you wake up one day and

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.080
decide to start a pest control company?

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.200
Not a tech startup or another real

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.800
estate company, but pest control. And

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.080
this was intentional. Some of the

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.080
biggest fortunes in America are built in

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.080
boring industries that most people

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.320
completely ignore. Things like pest

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.520
control, plumbing, HVAC, waste

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.040
management, and home service businesses

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.960
like pest control are some of the

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.000
fastest path to a nine-figure company

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.400
that pretty much nobody on YouTube is

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.160
talking about. So, today I want to show

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.640
you exactly why I'm building one and how

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.120
I'm doing it. And before we go any

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.960
further, at any point throughout the

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.880
video, if you get value, do me a huge

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.840
favor. Just go ahead and like it. It

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.560
helps me out a ton and it will help get

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.480
this video in front of more people who

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.960
need to see it. All right, so why pest

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.640
control? I've been in real estate for a

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.760
little bit over a decade and every

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.720
single time we get a home under contract

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.040
and we get thousands under contract a

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.440
year, they typically need a termite

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.000
inspection, okay? Most people opt for

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.760
that. And for years we were just

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.440
outsourcing it. We were giving money to

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.879
other pest control companies. And that

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.800
was just the beginning of it because my

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.240
real estate companies cash flow really

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.200
well, but the problem with my real

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.960
estate companies that I realized is that

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.320
they're not really sellable. You know,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.640
private equity, they're not really going

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:17.360
to come knock on my door and buy this

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.960
giant wholesaling operation that I

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.880
built. And as I got a little bit older,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.520
I started thinking about those two real

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.600
estate companies that I built a little

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.360
differently. Not really as the

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.320
destination, but more as the foundation.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.000
More of like a launching pad as an

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.840
entrepreneur. Because what they give me

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.840
is something that a lot of entrepreneurs

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.280
starting a business don't have, and that

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.360
is the ability to be patient. I can take

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.360
zero dollars out of Nectar Pest Control.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.240
I could operate that company at a loss,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.480
and I could be in debt on that company

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.920
for years if that's what it took.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.680
Because I have other companies

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.840
generating millions and millions of

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.960
dollars a year in revenue that give me

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.160
the runway. And that really changes

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.880
everything about how I'm able to build

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.680
what I'm doing. Most people starting a

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.040
business, they need it to work fast,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.840
right? Because they needed to pay their

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.479
bills. They have to live off the company

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.040
that they're starting. But, I don't have

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.200
that pressure because I've been building

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.000
for a decade already. And that patience

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.880
is one of the biggest advantages that I

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.760
have. So, I started digging into the

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.120
industry a little bit, and what I found

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.440
surprised me. So, the US pest control

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.040
industry crossed $26 billion in revenue

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.440
last year, and it is projected to

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.200
continue to climb. And over 32,000

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.120
companies are competing for that pie

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.880
right now. And here in Arizona, which

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.120
obviously is one of the fastest-growing

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.880
states in the country, the demand is

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.959
relentless. I mean, we have every bug

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.400
imaginable. We got scorpions, we have

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:37.200
termites, we have cockroaches,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.000
everything you could imagine. And for

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.480
most people, this is a need, not a want.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.480
The industry is dominated by mom-and-pop

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.320
operators who are running the same way

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.280
they were running 20 years ago. They've

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.320
got no systems, their branding's

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.560
terrible, they are not integrating

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.120
technology or AI in their business at

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.360
all. And outside of big companies like

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.400
Orkin and Terminix, nobody's really

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.360
shown up to actually play the game.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.480
These big national companies, they own

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.400
the brand awareness, and everybody else

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.880
is just kind of there, right? And that

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.440
gap is ultimately the opportunity. So, I

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.440
want to spend a minute talking about why

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.200
boring businesses win, because I think

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.320
it is genuinely one of the most

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.400
underrated ideas in entrepreneurship.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.400
They win because nobody's competing in

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.280
them the right way. When an industry

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:24.440
isn't cool, the talent doesn't flow in.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.360
The sophisticated operators don't show

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.000
up, right? Think about it. The VC money

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.360
doesn't show up. The best marketers

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.880
don't show up. And that means that if

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.280
you're willing to bring real systems and

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.040
real branding and real customer

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.400
experience into a boring industry, you

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.280
have an enormous competitive advantage

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.360
over people who have been coasting for

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.280
the last 20 years. Think about what

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.640
Rollins did. They own Orkin plus a

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.920
portfolio of 60 other pest control

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.600
brands. They serve over 2.8 million

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.760
customers through wholly owned

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:59.120
subsidiaries and franchises in 2024

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.040
alone. They completed 44 acquisitions.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.840
The best businesses in my opinion in

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.880
2026 are not the most exciting or sexy

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.720
ones. They're the ones with consistent

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.720
demand, recurring revenue, and low

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.480
customer acquisition cost. And pest

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.440
control happens to have all three.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.920
Nobody wakes up one day and decides that

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:18.160
they don't have a pest problem anymore,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.760
right? Once you get that customer, you

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.720
tend to keep them for a really long

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.840
time. And that is a fundamentally

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.960
different business than almost anything

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.560
that I've ever built as an entrepreneur.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.960
So, let's talk about how I'm actually

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.200
building Nectar, all right? So, the

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.560
strategy is really two-pronged, okay?

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.600
We're going to organically grow, and

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.120
we're going to do strategic acquisitions

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.960
along the way.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.960
And on the organic side, we're building

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.960
Nectar from the ground up, right? We're

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.680
in the Arizona market. We already have a

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.600
lot of customers, and we're building the

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.960
brand. We're building the systems. We're

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:51.440
building the customer experience. We're

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.680
really proving the model. The problem is

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.560
is that organic alone is extremely slow.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.680
So, simultaneously, I'm looking to

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.280
acquire smaller pest control companies.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.440
And there's a ton of them like we talked

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.600
about, and there's a lot of these

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.000
operators who built a pretty solid

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.000
customer base, right? They have anywhere

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.840
from 200 customers to 1,000 customers.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.640
They've got that recurring revenue, but

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.840
they don't have a succession plan. They

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.600
don't have anybody to take over, and a

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.480
lot of them are ready to walk away.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.400
They're getting old. And the strategy is

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.360
to acquire those accounts and that

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.280
revenue and essentially just bolt that

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.720
on to Nectar's infrastructure and

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.480
compress years of growth into

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.680
potentially months. You're not just

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.560
competing for customers one at a time,

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.600
you're buying people's entire books of

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.880
businesses. And once we've proven and

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.200
refined the model here in Arizona, then

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.400
the next move is obviously geographic

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.600
expansion. So, one of the markets that I

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.240
really like is Houston, Dallas, and

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.560
really just Texas in general. And all we

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.200
got to do is replicate that playbook and

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.520
continue to scale in those other

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.400
markets. So, let me break down my goals

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.680
and why multiple nine figures is

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.400
actually a really realistic target. So,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.240
first I want to make a distinction that

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.480
most people are not talking about.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.600
There's a big difference between a

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.960
hundred million dollars in revenue and a

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.680
hundred million dollar valuation. And I

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.240
won't necessarily need to hit a hundred

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.600
million in revenue to build a hundred

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.480
million dollar company. So, if you're

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.040
not familiar, pest control runs on

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.960
recurring revenue, okay? So, typically

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.720
customers sign up and they pay anywhere

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.600
between like forty and eighty dollars a

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.360
month, okay? Most plans probably range

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.360
between fifty and sixty bucks a month.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.280
But, to be conservative, let's just call

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.240
it fifty-five dollars a month and let's

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.640
say that equates to six hundred and

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.840
sixty dollars a year per customer. But,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.680
it's really not the whole picture

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.320
because we do a lot of one-time jobs,

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.960
too, that aren't recurring, right?

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.480
Termite treatments are obviously a

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.760
really big one. And that's not recurring

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.240
revenue, but it is significant revenue.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.640
And our goal, of course, over time is to

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.320
convert those one-time customers into

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.800
recurring customers, okay? We're not

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.800
going to get all of them, but the goal

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.200
is to get as many as we can. So, let's

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.560
talk about what a hundred million

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.600
dollars actually means in the context of

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.080
what we're talking about. So, if Nectar

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:01.480
gets to a hundred million in annual

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:03.840
revenue, let's just assume a thirty

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.040
percent profit margin. And let's assume

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.880
that when we go to sell, that private

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:11.080
equity companies are paying a twenty

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:13.640
times multiple on earnings or an EBITDA.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.600
We're looking potentially at a valuation

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.280
north of a half a billion dollars. But,

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.040
here's the thing, I don't necessarily

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:22.520
need to get to a hundred million in

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.480
revenue to have a nine-figure exit,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.200
depending on how many multiple nine

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.040
figures I'm looking for. See, if the

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.240
right buyer came along in, let's say,

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:32.120
five years and offered me three hundred

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.000
million for the company, I'm probably

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.680
going to entertain that call. Maybe I'll

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.440
sell, maybe I won't. So, I'm just

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.640
keeping it simple, I'm not diving too

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.880
deep into EBITDA and all the technical

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:43.560
stuff, okay? The point is is that

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:45.600
revenue is just one part of the

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.680
equation. Margins matter a lot, all

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.680
right? Your net income matters a lot.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.080
Multiples matter, right? What is PE

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.560
paying at that time? Nobody can predict

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.760
that, okay? PE was paying a crap load of

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:00.000
money during the COVID era, so we'll see

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:02.200
what they're paying in 2030 or 2032.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.720
Recurring revenue also matters more than

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:06.560
any one-time revenue when a buyer is

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.840
valuing your business. So, obviously our

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:11.120
goal is to get as much recurring revenue

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.960
as we possibly can. So, I'd be doing you

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.040
a disservice if I made all of this sound

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.240
easy, all right? It's not easy, but

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.000
here's what I've learned, okay? What

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.080
I've learned is the challenges in this

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.520
industry are actually what create the

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.520
opportunity.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.680
So, take these national brands in pest

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.360
control in particular, right? We're

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.320
talking about Orkin, we're talking about

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.200
Terminix. They've got big marketing

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.520
budgets and they've got decades of brand

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.400
recognition.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.039
And when a homeowner needs pest control,

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.960
a lot of those names come to their mind

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:39.159
first, right? And building brand

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.120
awareness from scratch in a market where

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:43.280
two or three giants already own the

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.040
customer's mind takes a lot of time and

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.839
freaking money. But big also means slow,

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:51.200
okay? Big means impersonal. Big means

00:08:48.839 --> 00:08:53.160
cutting corners to protect margins. And

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.240
that is exactly where people like us

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.960
come in, right? Most pest control

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.360
companies out there are doing the cheap

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.880
option, okay? They're backpack spraying.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.160
Their branding's terrible. They're

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.280
driving old beat-up trucks. They don't

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.920
look and feel premium at all. And

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.680
customers notice that, even if they

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.280
can't always articulate it, all right?

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:12.000
With Nectar, we took an entirely

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.640
different approach. We decided to use

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.880
power spraying equipment, okay? We put

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.800
down a lot more product and it is a lot

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.920
better of a service. We invest heavily

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:21.280
into our brand. Take a look at our

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:23.440
trucks, right? Custom wheels, beautiful

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:25.520
paint jobs. We invest in how we're

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.280
showing up to people's front door. And

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.800
that really is entrepreneurship, right?

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.240
We look at the inefficiencies in a

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.640
particular industry that we're getting

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.560
into, in this case a boring industry,

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.000
and we build something way better. And

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.520
really the second big challenge is

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.200
diseducating the customers on the value,

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:43.520
right? A lot of homeowners, they treat

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.360
pest control like a commodity. They want

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.920
the cheapest option. And when you're

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:49.720
building a premium brand, you have to do

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:52.160
a lot of the work helping people kind of

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:53.600
understand why your service is different

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.920
and worth paying for. And that's a

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.480
marketing challenge, right? And we're

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:58.680
working through that every single day.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.600
And I would argue that after 6 months of

